            <ArticleTitle>Stages of change in obesity and weight management: factorial structure of the Italian version of the University of Rhode Island Change Assessment Scale.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>361-367</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s40519-016-0289-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine the factorial structure of the University of Rhode Island Change Assessment Scale (IT-URICA) for weight management in a sample of Italian overweight and obese patients enrolled in a nutritional rehabilitation program.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">334 inpatients completed the translated and adjusted version of the IT-URICA at admission to the hospital. Psychometric testing included confirmatory factor analysis and internal consistency (Cronbach's α).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The IT-URICA for weight management was successfully translated into Italian, and the factorial analysis confirmed the four-factor solution of the commonly accepted version of the measure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">High levels of RTC are considered critical to the long-term success of weight management, and the IT-URICA may be an appropriate measure of motivational readiness for use among Italian overweight and obese patients. Its use is, therefore, recommended for clinical and research purposes.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
